# Historical assessment of the 110-page thesis

Historical review evidence for the hashed artifact below, not a current task
list or an assessment of the recovered 86-page candidate. Current source
navigation is in [thesis/README.md](../thesis/README.md). Findings remain
attributed to this assessment and may have been superseded.

Date: 2026-09-14  
Artifact assessed: `thesis/build/main.pdf`  
Artifact SHA-256: `5f799da24818861cd0aeb54964a65b4fb3df7e87a27c4fc31d8ef80fbfa57ead`  
Source revision inspected: `add4f51bd7024bd7f3826a87445a69d31a9bbd32`  
Length: 110 pages  
Working title: *Probing Viterbo's Conjecture*

## Executive verdict

**Recommendation: FAIL / return for major revision as a submission artifact. Do not submit this PDF as-is.**

This is not a verdict that the topic fails, that the project lacks mathematics,
or that a replacement research topic is needed. The opposite is more likely:
the HKO local-maximality theorem, if it survives expert audit, is a strong and
appropriate central result for a master's thesis, and the rotated-pentagon
profile is a credible second result. I found no elementary contradiction in
the main proof architecture during this assessment.

The present PDF nevertheless fails as a finished thesis for four cumulative
reasons:

1. **It explicitly disclaims final human review and makes authorial
   responsibility difficult to establish.** Page 2 says that the manuscript was substantially revised
   after the author's last personal verification and that the statement does
   not assert final review of the current candidate. It also says that the
   author cannot reliably identify who originated every idea, passage,
   program, or decision. Whatever the permissibility of the underlying AI
   workflow, this wording asks an examiner to accept a thesis that its author
   says he has not finally checked. That is an immediate submission blocker.
2. **The headline theorem is not yet sufficiently auditable from the thesis
   artifact.** Its logic is plausible and its exact certificate is present in
   the repository, but the decisive computation depends on hand-supplied
   geometric and derivative formulas whose correspondence is outside the Sage
   check. The PDF itself acknowledges this boundary. An expert still has to
   audit those formulas, the local chart/slice argument, and the executable
   correspondence. The living repository is not a frozen scholarly object.
3. **The thesis does not establish its scholarly position well enough.** A
   110-page research thesis renders only ten bibliography entries. The novel
   claims are not set against a serious related-work account, and standard
   numerical, optimization, statistical, and computer-assisted-proof methods
   are mostly uncited. The unused bibliography even contains a directly
   relevant paper on local maximizers, but the thesis never discusses it.
4. **The narrative treats a strong mathematical thesis as a project
   portfolio.** The main result starts only on page 59. Conditional algorithms,
   weak negative experiments, implementation assurance, repository state,
   visualization, and an AI regression case compete with the mathematical
   contribution instead of supporting it. The sentence-level prose is usually
   grammatical, but its audit-log vocabulary and repeated caveats make the
   manuscript laborious and obscure the intellectual point.

My confidence is about 90% that this PDF should not be handed to an examiner
unchanged. My confidence is also about 80% that the topic and most of the
mathematical core should be retained rather than replaced. New mathematical
research is not the first prescription; verification, positioning, selection,
and rewriting are.

## What a final review should judge

The following review surface is broader than “find one fatal defect.” A
replacement workflow must close every row that matters to passing.

| Review dimension | Pass question | Current assessment |
|---|---|---|
| Formal admissibility | Is this recognizably a final master's-thesis submission with the required front matter, declarations, metadata, and archive? | **Fail / unknown.** The title page does not identify the university, program, degree, supervisor, or thesis type; the PDF metadata has blank title and author fields; no frozen release is identified. |
| Authorship and academic integrity | Can the author truthfully take responsibility for every submitted claim, and is the disclosed AI use permitted? | **Hard fail in the current wording.** Page 2 explicitly withholds final review of the current candidate and blurs intellectual origin. Institutional permissibility remains unverified. |
| Topic and research question | Is the topic mathematically substantive, bounded, and suitable for the degree? | **Pass.** Rigidity of the HKO counterexample and finite computation of EHZ capacity form a strong topic. |
| Original contribution | Is it explicit which results are new, nontrivial, and the author's? | **Promising but not established.** The local theorem and pentagon profile appear to be the contributions, but novelty and authorial ownership are not adequately documented. |
| Correctness | Are definitions, theorems, proofs, computations, and empirical claims correct? | **Not certified by this review.** No obvious collapse was found, but the two computer-assisted theorems need an expert trust-chain audit. |
| Proof completeness | Can a qualified reader follow why every headline conclusion follows, including the computer-assisted steps? | **Major revision.** The high-level implication is explained, but key formula-to-code and certificate-to-geometry obligations live outside the PDF. |
| Use of sources | Are imported results attributed accurately and is relevant literature covered? | **Fail.** Ten rendered references are not credible coverage for the breadth claimed. Related-work and method citations are missing. |
| Narrative | Does every chapter advance one intelligible thesis-level argument? | **Fail.** There is a recoverable spine, but the current whole is a portfolio with multiple weak side stories. |
| Exposition | Can the intended mathematical reader understand motivation, definitions, proof strategy, and significance without reconstructing the repository? | **Major revision.** Local explanations are often careful; global cognitive load is too high. |
| Sentence-level writing | Is the prose correct, direct, and readable? | **Borderline pass locally; fail globally.** Grammar is usually good, but the voice is bureaucratic, repetitive, and overqualified. |
| Empirical methodology | Do experiments support appropriately stated conclusions with reproducible provenance? | **Honest but weak.** The claims are bounded correctly, yet several experiments use uncertified historical evaluators or incomplete provenance and cannot carry the thesis. |
| Reproducibility | Are proof and data artifacts stable, available, and sufficient to repeat the claims? | **Fail for final release.** The PDF says the repository is living, inputs are missing, some producer states are unreconstructable, and there is no frozen DOI/archive. |
| Visual and typographic quality | Is the PDF polished and easy to navigate? | **Mixed.** Figures and equations are generally clean; front matter, pagination, section starts, and code placement are not submission quality. |
| Proportion and degree fit | Is the amount and kind of material appropriate for a master's thesis rather than a lab notebook or software report? | **Fail in present selection.** There is enough strong material, but too much secondary material and operational detail. |

## The diagnosis in detail

### 1. The topic is not failing

The title is broad, but the best underlying question is precise and worthwhile:
is the Haim--Kislev--Ostrover pentagon product locally maximal for the
normalized EHZ capacity once natural symmetries are removed? A theorem showing
local maximality in the ten-facet stratum would be a substantial master's-level
contribution. The exact rotated-pentagon profile is a natural, intelligible
companion result. The finite reduction to facet words gives the project a
coherent technical language.

This matters operationally: agents should **not** respond to the present FAIL
by inventing a new topic or by adding more experiments. The first question is
whether the existing central theorem is correct and presentable. If it is, the
project already has enough mathematical substance.

### 2. The authorship disclosure blocks submission before mathematics is judged

Page 2 contains two different disclosures:

- AI agents participated in choosing questions and methods and in developing
  arguments, software, experiments, prose, interpretation, criticism, and
  review.
- The author cannot reliably say who first originated every idea, passage,
  program, or decision and does not claim sole intellectual origin of every
  part.
- The author personally verified a July version, the manuscript was revised
  substantially later, and the disclosure expressly does not say that the
  current candidate received final review.

The third point is unambiguously fatal for a final candidate. The first two may
or may not be permissible under the applicable examination rules and the
advisor's approval, but they create a serious degree-authorship risk. Advisor
approval of broad AI use is not automatically equivalent to the examination
board accepting delegated intellectual production.

Required closure is not cosmetic rewriting. Before submission, Jörn must
personally review and be able to defend the complete final text, the institution
must permit the disclosed workflow, and the disclosure must say exactly what
responsibility he accepts. If those conditions cannot be met, the thesis has a
fundamental authorship problem regardless of mathematical quality.

The separate “AI in the Research Process” chapter on pages 97–99 does not solve
this problem. It analyzes one reconstructed code-repair episode with one run
per condition and unavailable original prompts. Its conclusion—that a test
should distinguish the defect it purports to catch—is sensible but slight,
not integrated with the symplectic-geometric thesis, and not evidence of human
mastery of the mathematical claims. Unless explicitly required, it should be
removed or reduced to an appendix note.

### 3. The mathematical core is plausible, but the current trust chain is not closed

#### 3.1 HKO local maximality, pages 59–66

The proof architecture is mathematically intelligible:

1. nearby ten-facet polytopes are placed in a 40-dimensional labelled
   polar-vertex chart;
2. the 15-dimensional translation/scaling/symplectic orbit is split off;
3. 26 smooth feasible sections give upper functions touching the systolic
   ratio at HKO;
4. their derivative rows annihilate the orbit directions, span the
   25-dimensional quotient cotangent space, and have a strictly positive
   convex relation summing to zero;
5. compactness of the unit sphere and uniform Taylor control give strict
   first-order decrease on the transverse slice.

That implication is credible. The certificate packet also records 26 rows,
rank 25, symmetry rank 15, positive coefficients, and exact annihilation.

What remains insufficiently established for a final thesis is the boundary
between the hand proof and the executable check. Page 65 correctly admits that
exact substitution does not prove that the hand-supplied HKO geometry,
volume-variation formulas, and derivative formulas represent the intended
polytope. The verifier also does not prove the Hausdorff-local chart, local
group slice, upper-function implication, or Taylor argument. These are normal
limitations of a computer-assisted proof, but the thesis needs a recorded
expert audit of precisely those interfaces. At present the reader is directed
to a mutable repository packet rather than given a frozen certificate with a
stable citation and a compact independent verification protocol.

This review found no simple counterexample to the upper-function argument. It
did **not** independently rederive all 40-coordinate derivatives, audit every
Sage predicate against the geometry, or validate the local group action. The
proper status is therefore “strong theorem candidate requiring focused expert
verification,” not either “known false” or “already safe.”

#### 3.2 Rotated-pentagon profile, pages 75–90

This result has a clearer reader-facing proof: symmetry reduces the parameter
range; a displayed six-facet branch supplies one inequality; a finite block
enumeration supplies a competitor family; the exact classifier proves action
gaps on the open cells; continuity closes exceptional parameters. One
competitor is worked by hand, which materially improves credibility.

The remaining risk is again correspondence and archival stability. The proof
relies on a 3340-word Sage classification and on the correctness of the
generator/pruning interface. The document says that a floating-point producer
and exact certificate agree on the word count but that their word sets were not
independently compared. That does not itself break the proof, because the
floating-point producer is said not to be a proof input, but it is a needless
reader alarm. The exact generator and its mathematical coverage are what must
be audited and frozen.

Six pages of cleaned Sage excerpts (pages 85–90) are the wrong compromise: too
long for readers who trust the certificate, yet not enough to make the proof
independent of the full source. Move the full executable and explained listing
to a stable supplement. Keep only pseudocode, the mathematical predicates, a
hash/version, outcome counts, and reproduction instructions in the thesis.

#### 3.3 Conditional flow-graph algorithm, pages 42–51

The chapter states an idealized exact real-arithmetic theorem under explicit
regularity hypotheses and a rational corollary. This is honest and potentially
useful, but it is not needed for either headline theorem. Its chamber-genericity
argument and fixed-point semantics are novel enough to deserve specialist
review, while its present role in the story is merely “a complementary
algorithm.” It therefore incurs high proof and reading cost for low narrative
return.

Unless the algorithm is independently important to the degree claim, move it
to an appendix or a separate paper. If retained as a main contribution, give it
a motivating example, compare it more explicitly with Chaidez–Hutchings and
the Haim–Kislev route, and obtain expert review of the genericity proof.

#### 3.4 Foundations and finite reduction, pages 10–58

The order—preliminaries, generalized Reeb orbits, Haim–Kislev QP,
first-order variation—is broadly sensible. The text is unusually careful about
distinguishing dual curves, boundary orbits, feasible words, KKT stationary
points, fixed-word maxima, and global maxima. That is a strength.

The cost is 49 pages before the main theorem. Some of this material reproves or
repackages published results. The thesis must distinguish clearly between:

- standard definitions and cited facts;
- reproofs included for convention checking;
- genuinely new lemmas needed by the headline theorem;
- implementation contracts that belong in documentation rather than the
  mathematical narrative.

Without this distinction, length looks like contribution and a reader cannot
tell what is being examined.

### 4. Novelty and literature positioning are inadequate

The rendered bibliography occupies only parts of pages 109–110 and contains
ten items. For a thesis spanning symplectic capacities, generalized Reeb
orbits, nonsmooth convex analysis, Minkowski billiards, local extremality,
quadratic programming, verified floating-point computation, data science, and
computer-assisted proof, this is not enough.

Specific gaps include:

- no serious related-work subsection for local maximizers of capacity ratios;
- no discussion of the directly relevant Baracco–Bernardi–Lange–Mazzucchelli
  item already present but uncited in `bibliography.bib`;
- little historical sourcing for Ekeland–Hofer–Zehnder capacity and generalized
  characteristics;
- almost no citations for CMA-ES, bootstrap intervals, PCA, clustering,
  random forests, interval arithmetic, verified linear algebra, or
  computer-assisted proof practice;
- no explicit novelty table saying which theorem/algorithm/experiment is new,
  which is an adaptation, and which is imported.

This is content-level work. Adding citations mechanically is not enough. The
introduction must explain what was known, what exact gap the thesis closes, why
the result is nontrivial, and how it compares in strength and scope with nearby
literature.

### 5. The narrative has a strong spine that the current structure obscures

The recoverable core story is:

> HKO disproved Viterbo's conjecture with a pentagon product. Finite facet-word
> descriptions make its capacity tractable. First-order feasible upper
> functions then prove local rigidity in the ten-facet stratum, while an exact
> one-parameter computation shows global optimality within the rotated regular
> pentagon family.

The current table of contents dilutes that story:

- the headline theorem starts on page 59;
- a conditional flow-graph algorithm occupies pages 42–51 without feeding the
  theorem;
- the bounded negative data-science chapter occupies pages 67–74 and a
  substantial appendix;
- six pages of Sage code interrupt the pentagon theorem;
- visualization gets its own chapter for one figure and a negative result;
- numerics and repository availability read like software assurance reports;
- an unrelated AI testing case receives a standalone chapter immediately
  before the conclusion.

The introduction says the project “probes” the counterexample from several
directions. That accurately describes a portfolio, but a thesis needs a
hierarchy, not equal billing for every completed workstream. The main theorem
and exact family result should dominate; everything else should be prerequisite,
evidence, limitation, appendix, or omitted.

A plausible replacement structure is:

1. Introduction, related work, and explicit contributions.
2. Minimal symplectic/convex preliminaries and the finite facet-word capacity
   formula.
3. First-order upper functions in facet coordinates.
4. Main theorem: HKO local maximality, including the computer-assisted proof
   protocol.
5. Exact rotated-pentagon profile.
6. Short computational perspective: what was searched, what was not found,
   and what the exact results imply.
7. Conclusion and precise open problems.
8. Appendices/supplement: flow graph, numerical contracts, code excerpts,
   data tables, AI-process material if required.

This is not a demand for a shorter thesis at all costs. It is a demand that
page allocation express intellectual importance.

### 6. The writing is locally competent but globally exhausting

The draft is generally grammatical, mathematically literate, and careful with
scope. Definitions are usually introduced before use, claims are often bounded
honestly, and the prose avoids obvious hype. The abstract does identify the
main results.

The dominant weakness is that the voice resembles an internal audit or agent
handoff rather than a mathematical thesis. Examples of recurring vocabulary
are “proof-facing packet,” “retained manifest,” “living repository,” “candidate
source,” “typed rejection,” “bounded negative result,” and “evidence boundary.”
An automatic count over the active TeX found approximately 30,000 words, with
163 uses of “finite,” 99 of “exact,” 55 of “retained,” 34 of “evidence,” and
207 of “not.” Counts are not defects by themselves, but they match the reading
experience: the text repeatedly tells the reader what it does not prove instead
of developing the central mathematical idea.

The qualification is often scientifically responsible. The problem is its
placement and repetition. State a clean theorem or empirical question first;
give the key reason and result; collect limitations once at the end of the
section. Do not force every paragraph to carry provenance, trust-boundary, and
non-claim language.

Other writing problems:

- long sentences frequently contain four or more logical qualifications;
- abstract and introduction enumerate too many secondary results;
- implementation state is written in the present tense (“current
  implementation,” “at the time of writing”), making the thesis age like a
  status report;
- first-person plural appears in empirical chapters without a stable account
  of who “we” denotes;
- significance is often replaced by process precision: the reader learns how
  evidence is classified before learning why the result matters.

This is not “bad English.” It is a genre and information-design failure.
Sentence polishing alone will not repair it; selection and paragraph-level
rewriting must come first.

### 7. The empirical chapters are honest but cannot carry much degree value

The thesis is commendably explicit that the random and data-science searches
do not prove nonexistence. However, the main negative table uses one seed, one
height interval, fixed facet-count ranges, and a maximum around 0.863—far from
the HKO value around 1.047. The generated-candidate experiment evaluates 1675
selected-or-baseline rows with a historical, uncertified evaluator. The local
optimizer comparison also uses that evaluator, unequal realized compute, and
imperfectly established holdout lineage. One selected-body screen cannot be
exactly reproduced because its dirty producer state was not retained.

These limitations are disclosed correctly, but disclosure does not create a
scientific contribution. The useful empirical conclusions are modest:

- the specified generators did not rediscover or improve HKO;
- invariant descriptors explain some within-table variation;
- one finite-budget branch-history method outperformed six alternatives on a
  named historical objective;
- sparse local probes can miss thin improving cones.

That can support a short “computational search and lessons” section. It should
not compete with the local theorem. More generic machine learning is unlikely
to improve the thesis unless it is driven by a precise mathematical hypothesis
and evaluated prospectively with a certified capacity route.

### 8. Reproducibility is described honestly but is not final-release quality

Pages 96–97 say that the public repository is living, not an immutable release;
that twelve external snapshots are absent from intended release paths; that a
plain clone cannot repeat every empirical analysis or regenerate every figure;
and that there is no Zenodo record or DOI. Pages 73–74 also state that one
producer's dirty source state cannot be reconstructed.

Honesty is good, but the final artifact needs a stable evidentiary target. At a
minimum:

- freeze the exact source revision used for the PDF;
- archive the two theorem-bearing verifier packets and their outputs;
- record software versions, commands, hashes, and expected success summaries;
- either archive empirical inputs needed for claims or reduce/remove claims
  that cannot be reproduced;
- cite the frozen object from the thesis.

The thesis need not preserve every experiment ever run. It must preserve the
inputs and checks needed for claims that survive into the final text.

### 9. Visual presentation is clean in detail but not in composition

The build is technically clean: a fresh build passes the repository's selected
PDF, overfull-box, and reference checks. Equations and figures are generally
sharp, fonts are consistent, and no overlapping or clipped content was found in
the full-page visual scan.

Submission-level defects remain:

- page 1 combines a minimal title block with the entire abstract and begins
  Arabic page numbering;
- the title page lacks institutional and degree information;
- PDF title and author metadata are blank, and the PDF is untagged;
- page 9 contains only the final sentence of the introduction;
- section 5 begins at the bottom of page 42 after the end of section 4;
- section 10 begins immediately below the end of a code listing on page 91;
- section 13 begins at the bottom of page 97;
- the long section-5 title breaks awkwardly in the table of contents;
- several figure/table-only or low-content pages create an uneven rhythm;
- code listings occupy pages that should present mathematics, not source
  transcription.

These are readily repairable after the structure is fixed. They should not be
fixed first, because re-pagination of the present structure would be wasted
work.

## What content-level work is actually needed

Content-level work is needed, but “do more research” is not yet justified.

### Mandatory mathematical work

1. **Independent audit of the HKO theorem interfaces.** Re-derive or verify the
   HKO dual vertices, volume and first variation, feasible-section derivatives,
   orbit tangent rank, quotient slice, and the exact formula-to-Sage mapping.
2. **Independent audit of the pentagon enumeration.** Verify that the exact
   generator covers the mathematically proved block family, that every pruning
   predicate is necessary on the open domain, and that exceptional parameters
   are closed correctly by continuity.
3. **Novelty review.** Compare the headline results with HKO, Haim–Kislev,
   Chaidez–Hutchings, Rudolf, and the local-maximizer literature. State exactly
   what is new.
4. **Claim freeze.** Decide which of the flow-graph theorem, numerical
   implementation theorem, and empirical results are degree contributions and
   which are appendices or external project outputs.

### Mandatory authoring work

1. Rewrite the introduction around one central question and a ranked
   contribution list.
2. Cut, move, or compress material that does not advance that question.
3. Replace repository/process language with mathematical exposition.
4. Add a proper related-work discussion and source every imported method.
5. Make the computer-assisted proof protocol self-contained enough to audit
   and cite a frozen supplement.
6. Rewrite the conclusion to interpret the mathematics rather than inventory
   workstreams.
7. Only then repair front matter, section breaks, metadata, and page layout.

### Work that should not be started by default

- a new thesis topic;
- more undirected random search;
- a larger collection of machine-learning methods;
- more code excerpts in the PDF;
- line-editing all 110 pages before deciding what survives;
- typography-only repairs to chapters likely to move to appendices.

## A pass-oriented workflow and gates

The following sequence is designed to prevent an agent from fixing one visible
problem while leaving the next failure untouched.

### Gate 0: admissibility and responsibility

Owner: Jörn plus the relevant institutional authority.  
Pass evidence:

- the AI workflow and disclosure are permitted;
- Jörn has personally reviewed the exact final candidate and accepts
  responsibility for every claim;
- required title-page, declaration, and submission elements are known.

If this gate fails, no amount of prose improvement makes the document
submittable. Mathematical auditing can still proceed because it is useful
under either outcome.

### Gate 1: theorem truth and novelty

Owner: mathematically qualified reviewer independent of the producing agents.  
Pass evidence:

- a written obligation-by-obligation audit of both headline theorems;
- successful reruns of frozen certificates;
- explicit comparison with the relevant literature;
- a stable statement of what is original.

If a headline theorem fails here, weaken or remove it before redesigning the
narrative. If both survive, do not add a new headline result.

### Gate 2: scope selection

Owner: Jörn as final internal judge.  
Pass evidence:

- a one-page thesis claim map with one main result, at most two secondary
  contributions, prerequisites, and appendices;
- a decision for every current chapter: keep, compress, move, or remove;
- a target reader and assumed prerequisites.

Expected recommendation: keep HKO local maximality as the center; keep the
pentagon profile; retain only the finite machinery those results need; compress
the empirical search; move flow graph, detailed numerics, code, data tables,
and AI-process discussion out of the main line unless the examiner explicitly
values them.

### Gate 3: structural rewrite

Pass evidence:

- the introduction states the problem, state of the art, gap, contribution,
  significance, and limitations;
- every main-line chapter is necessary for a headline claim;
- the main theorem appears early enough that the reader knows what the
  foundations are building toward;
- each chapter opens with purpose and closes with the mathematical takeaway,
  not a provenance inventory.

### Gate 4: proof exposition and reproducibility

Pass evidence:

- a reader can trace every theorem premise to a definition, lemma, citation,
  or finite certificate;
- certificate inputs, source hashes, software versions, commands, and outputs
  are frozen and cited;
- no empirical claim relies on unavailable inputs or an unreconstructable
  producer state;
- code in the main body is limited to the smallest pseudocode needed to
  understand a proof obligation.

### Gate 5: sentence and page quality

Pass evidence:

- a cold mathematical reader can summarize each section after one read;
- qualifications are consolidated and repetition removed;
- notation, citations, figures, captions, and cross-references are checked;
- front matter and declarations meet the actual rules;
- no orphan pages or bottom-of-page major section starts remain;
- PDF metadata, bookmarks/accessibility expectations, and archive links are
  correct.

### Gate 6: final human acceptance

Pass evidence:

- Jörn reads the frozen PDF, not only changed fragments;
- he records concrete acceptance or defects against this review matrix;
- any further change invalidates that acceptance until its affected scope is
  reviewed again;
- the final examiner-facing artifact and its frozen supplement are identical
  to the accepted versions.

## Minimum credible route to PASS

Assuming the two headline theorems survive expert audit, the minimum credible
route is a substantial rewrite rather than a replacement thesis:

1. resolve AI/admissibility and personal-responsibility language;
2. obtain a focused independent proof and novelty audit;
3. restructure around HKO local rigidity and the pentagon profile;
4. reduce the data-science, implementation, visualization, repository, and AI
   material to supporting roles;
5. freeze and cite the theorem-bearing artifacts;
6. complete an examiner-style full read and formal submission check.

That route changes content selection, claims, citations, and exposition. It is
not a proofreading pass. It also does not require a new topic unless the proof
audit defeats the local theorem.

## Limits of this assessment

This assessment included a complete page-level visual scan, text extraction,
source inspection across all active chapters, a fresh clean thesis build, an
inventory of theorem and citation surfaces, and inspection of the two exact
certificate packets and recorded outputs. It did not:

- independently rederive every theorem or execute the 44-minute pentagon
  certificate;
- complete a line-by-line source-to-Sage correspondence proof;
- verify current university regulations or advisor/examination-board approval
  of the AI workflow;
- establish priority or novelty from a comprehensive external literature
  search;
- judge oral defensibility by interviewing the author.

Accordingly, “no elementary contradiction found” must not be promoted to
“mathematically verified,” and “submission blocker” must not be misread as
“research topic rejected.”
